## Your rights at work

MCAS can count as a disability under the **Equality Act 2010** if it has a **substantial, long-term effect** on your daily life. This includes conditions with symptoms that **come and go**. Your employer must then consider **reasonable adjustments**, such as:

- a **fragrance-free** area, or a desk away from kitchens, smoke and chemicals
- control over **heating, fans or air conditioning**
- **flexible hours** or working from home on bad days
- extra **breaks**, and somewhere to rest
- time off for **appointments**
- a plan for what to do if you have a **flare or anaphylaxis** at work

**Access to Work** may help pay for support or equipment you need at work. Read about [Access to Work](/support/work-and-study/access-to-work/).

## Benefits

PIP is based on how MCAS affects you on **most days**, not on the diagnosis itself. For example, it may affect:

- **preparing food and eating:** if you need to follow a strict diet or have reactions to cooking smells
- **washing and bathing:** if heat triggers flushing, dizziness or fainting
- **managing medicines:** if you need help with a complex medicine routine or adrenaline auto-injectors
- **moving around and planning journeys:** if reactions, dizziness or fainting make going out unsafe

If your symptoms change a lot, describe your **bad days** and how often they happen, and what happens **after** a flare.

| Your age | Benefit to look at |
| --- | --- |
| Under 16 | [DLA for children](/benefits/dla/) |
| 16 to State Pension age | [Personal Independence Payment (PIP)](/benefits/pip/) |
| State Pension age or over | [Attendance Allowance](/benefits/attendance-allowance/) |

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you cannot work because of your health, read about [New Style ESA](/benefits/esa/) and the [Universal Credit health element](/benefits/universal-credit-health/).

## Prescriptions

MCAS can mean taking several medicines. In England, a **prescription prepayment certificate** covers all your prescriptions for a fixed price. It saves money if you need **4 or more items** in 3 months, or **12 or more items** in a year. Prescriptions are **free in Wales**. Read about [free NHS prescriptions](/support/health-costs/prescriptions/).
